      en: "Balboa is a historic port town located at the Pacific entrance of the Panama Canal, within the Panama City metropolitan area. Named after the Spanish explorer Vasco Nuñez de Balboa, it was built by the United States during the canal's construction to serve as the administrative center for the former Canal Zone. The town's architecture reflects this history, with many American-style buildings and wide, tree-lined avenues like the iconic Prado. Balboa is home to one of the busiest container ports in Latin America, playing a vital role in global maritime trade. It also serves as a major hub for the Panama Canal Railway and features important landmarks such as the Panama Canal Administration Building, which sits atop a hill overlooking the canal operations. Balboa offers a unique perspective on the intersection of industrial engineering, international logistics, and colonial history in Panama. History K8 — the Panama Canal construction."
